{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_false-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-2_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Sourness in wine is primarily due to its natural acidity, which varies depending on grape variety, climate, and production methods. White wines are generally more acidic than reds, especially those not aged in wood barrels. Among white wines, Sauvignon Blanc\u2014particularly dry-hopped styles\u2014offers vibrant citrus and tropical fruit notes, resulting in a refreshing tartness. Chardonnay is another popular white; while often bold and dry, its acidity can be balanced by oak aging.\n\nFor red wines, certain varietals can also have a noticeable tartness. Wines like Zinfandel, Petite Sirah, Nero d\u2019Avola, and Merlot can range in acidity, with Zinfandel and Nero d\u2019Avola often displaying more pronounced sour notes, especially in cooler climates.\n\nIf you are interested in exploring sourness in wine itself rather than through cocktails, focus on varietals and regions known for higher acidity and tart profiles. This approach will let you enjoy the sour characteristics naturally present in the wine, echoing some traditional preferences in winemaking, including those found in older European wineries.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0013-16-04303:2": 23.085527420043945, "clueweb22-en0021-83-07168:12": 22.898408889770508, "clueweb22-en0037-60-09924:21": 22.878501892089844, "clueweb22-en0001-75-05599:15": 22.801637649536133, "clueweb22-en0016-73-13509:12": 22.715951919555664, "clueweb22-en0017-19-06857:13": 22.686222076416016, "clueweb22-en0040-18-06522:7": 22.601036071777344, "clueweb22-en0009-54-06386:5": 22.36300277709961, "clueweb22-en0031-83-05665:8": 22.346708297729492, "clueweb22-en0036-03-04971:1": 22.21312141418457}, "ptkb_provenance": ["My grandfather used to have a winery in Great Britain."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0013-16-04303:2": 23.085527420043945, "clueweb22-en0021-83-07168:12": 22.898408889770508,