{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_false-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Certainly. The anatomy of the finger relevant to climbing injuries primarily includes the flexor tendons and their sheaths, as well as the annular pulleys. The flexor tendons run along the palm side of your fingers and allow you to bend your finger joints. These tendons are held close to the bone by structures called annular pulleys, preventing the tendon from bowstringing away from the bone when you grip.\n\nClimbers often experience injuries to these structures due to the intense load placed on the fingers when gripping small holds. A common tendon injury, sometimes called \u201cclimber\u2019s finger,\u201d happens when the tendon sheath is strained or damaged, often felt as pain along the palm side of the finger, sometimes with swelling or a \u201cpop.\u201d\n\nIf your pain is mild and specific to certain movements, it could indicate a strain rather than a rupture. Rest, moderation, gentle movement, and gradual return to climbing are important. If you experience persistent pain, swelling, or inability to bend the finger, it is best to seek medical evaluation. Proper warm-up, grip technique, and progressive training can help prevent future injuries.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0045-41-08747:9": 25.157543182373047, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 25.06287956237793, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:2": 24.836706161499023, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:11": 24.584604263305664, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 24.546512603759766, "clueweb22-en0006-07-05326:0": 24.470741271972656, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:19": 24.389774322509766, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 24.202932357788086, "clueweb22-en0008-84-09982:21": 24.154497146606445, "clueweb22-en0044-90-18231:0": 24.08716583251953}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."]}], "refe