{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_false-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Certainly. The location of your finger pain can provide important clues about the type of injury you might be experiencing, especially as a regular outdoor climber:\n\n- **Pulley Injuries:** If the pain is at the base of your finger, especially near the palm, and worsens when you try to crimp or apply pressure, it could indicate a pulley injury. These are common in climbers due to the forces involved in gripping holds.\n\n- **Tendon (Flexor Tendon) Pain:** If the pain feels deep and runs along the underside of your finger, particularly when you curl or flex your finger, the flexor tendon might be involved. Such injuries can result from overuse or sudden, intense loading\u2014both frequent in harder routes. You may also notice swelling or a \"creaking\" sensation.\n\n- **Joint (Capsular or Ligament) Pain:** If the pain is on the sides of the finger joints or feels more like a general ache, it could be a capsular or ligament strain. This is less common but can occur, especially if you feel pain during side-to-side movements.\n\n- **Muscle Strain:** If the pain is more diffuse and occurs after a session with significant fatigue, especially involving open-handed grips, it might be a muscle strain affecting the small hand muscles.\n\nIf your pain is persistent, worsening, or accompanied by numbness or loss of movement, it is advisable to seek medical evaluation for an accurate diagnosis and treatment plan.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0008-84-09982:22": 24.48605728149414, "clueweb22-en0005-98-01404:21": 23.98323631286621, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 23.953214645385742, "clueweb22-en0013-93-15759:18": 23.772794723510742, "clueweb22-en0016-25-19413:13": 23.772794723510742, "clueweb22-en0038-00-07527:13": 23.74152946472168, "clueweb22-en0001-34-18428:18": 23.711732864379883, "clueweb22-en0038-33-02567:13": 23.70360183715820