{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_false-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Based on your description\u2014pain in the front of the finger, especially when crimping, and no visible bowstringing\u2014it is still possible that you have an A2 pulley injury, even if it is not a complete rupture. Partial strains or microtears of the A2 pulley are common among climbers and can present with pain during crimping but may not always show obvious bowstringing or severe swelling.\n\nA2 pulley injuries are most often associated with pain at the base of the finger, particularly during movements that load the flexor tendons, such as crimping. Bowstringing typically appears with more severe or complete ruptures; in partial injuries, it may not be present.\n\nTo support recovery, experts recommend taping techniques (such as H-taping) to keep the tendon close to the bone and protect the pulley when climbing. Additionally, avoiding activities that provoke pain\u2014especially crimping\u2014and allowing adequate rest (often at least six weeks for proper healing) are important steps.\n\nIf pain persists, worsens, or you are unsure about the severity, clinical assessment and imaging (ultrasound or MRI) can help confirm the diagnosis and guide further management.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 43.29631805419922, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 37.310176849365234, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 35.865169525146484, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 34.272254943847656, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:3": 33.27394104003906, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 32.720420837402344, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:6": 32.6212158203125, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 32.525238037109375, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:18": 32.50815963745117, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:4": 32.12206268310547}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 43