{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_false-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Given your pain is in the front of the finger, especially during crimping, and you do not observe bowstringing, it is likely a strain rather than a full rupture of the A2 pulley. The recommended next steps are:\n\n1. Rest: Avoid climbing and any activities that cause pain, especially crimping, for at least a couple of weeks to allow healing.\n2. Ice: Apply ice to the affected area for 10-15 minutes several times a day to reduce inflammation.\n3. Gentle Movement: After a few days, begin gentle, pain-free range-of-motion exercises to prevent stiffness.\n4. Gradual Return: Only return to climbing when you are pain-free during daily activities and can press on the finger without discomfort. Start with easier routes and avoid crimping initially.\n5. Prevention: Consider warming up thoroughly and incorporating finger strengthening and stretching exercises into your routine.\n\nIf pain persists, worsens, or you develop swelling or bowstringing, consult a healthcare professional for further evaluation.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0031-57-03794:8": 10.423240661621094, "clueweb22-en0031-57-03794:9": 10.386695861816406, "clueweb22-en0006-14-14126:6": 10.066540718078613, "clueweb22-en0012-24-15587:1": 10.058679580688477, "clueweb22-en0035-62-11994:11": 9.909998893737793, "clueweb22-en0024-57-11018:0": 9.890768051147461, "clueweb22-en0009-74-14764:22": 9.845183372497559, "clueweb22-en0014-91-10483:6": 9.7752685546875, "clueweb22-en0038-39-06790:2": 9.668920516967773, "clueweb22-en0033-33-09501:2": 9.658951759338379}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0031-57-03794:8": 10.423240661621094, "clueweb22-en0031-57-03794:9": 10.386695861816406, "clueweb22-en0006-14-14126:6": 10.066540718078613, "clueweb22-en0012-24-15587:1": 10.058679580688477, "cluewe