{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_false-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "That is a wise decision. When you begin climbing again after a few weeks of rest, it is important to approach your return gradually to avoid re-injury and support proper healing. Here are some key recommendations:\n\n1. **Gentle Reintroduction**: Start with easy routes and lower intensity sessions. Pay attention to how your finger responds and avoid movements that cause pain, especially crimps.\n2. **Progressive Loading**: Once pain-free motion returns, gradually build up finger strength. Incorporate progressive hangboarding routines and gentle re-strengthening exercises, but only as tolerated.\n3. **Taping**: Properly tape the injured finger during sessions to provide extra support and help prevent excessive strain.\n4. **Warm-Up Thoroughly**: Always do a comprehensive warm-up with finger, hand, and arm exercises before climbing or hangboarding.\n5. **Avoid Overexertion**: Don\u2019t rush back to harder grades. Gradually increase difficulty as your finger feels stronger.\n6. **Listen to Your Body**: Recognize pain as a sign to rest. If pain returns, reduce intensity or take additional rest.\n7. **Preventative Care**: Use massage and trigger point techniques to reduce scar tissue formation, and incorporate stretching and general arm health exercises.\n\nTaking these steps should help minimize the risk of re-injury and support your continued climbing progress.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0005-98-01404:20": 21.74204444885254, "clueweb22-en0013-93-15759:16": 21.118303298950195, "clueweb22-en0038-00-07527:11": 21.118303298950195, "clueweb22-en0016-25-19413:11": 21.118303298950195, "clueweb22-en0038-33-02567:12": 21.04595375061035, "clueweb22-en0001-34-18428:17": 21.043554306030273, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 20.815473556518555, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:11": 20.693382263183594, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 20.655