{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_false-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The H-taping method is currently regarded as the most effective taping technique for supporting injured finger pulleys, particularly in climbers. Research indicates that H-taping provides superior support to the tendon and helps prevent bowstringing compared to other methods such as ring taping or X-taping. \n\nTo use H-taping, a strip of tape is cut into the shape of an \u201cH.\u201d The central part of the H is placed over the injured pulley area (usually the front of the finger), while the two side \u201clegs\u201d wrap around the sides of the finger, anchoring the tape. This configuration helps keep the tendon close to the bone, reduces the load on the injured pulley during climbing movements, and can even increase crimping strength during recovery.\n\nIt is important to note that taping should only be used to support an existing injury\u2014not preventatively on healthy fingers\u2014as taping healthy fingers offers no meaningful protection and may even hinder tendon and pulley strength development.\n\nIf you choose to use H-taping, ensure the tape is snug but not overly tight, and monitor for any discomfort or restricted blood flow. Resume climbing gradually and keep monitoring your symptoms for any changes.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 10.568115234375, "clueweb22-en0034-09-06168:1": 10.539856910705566, "clueweb22-en0004-58-05093:2": 10.001769065856934, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": 9.971715927124023, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 9.870238304138184, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 9.629443168640137, "clueweb22-en0004-58-05093:3": 9.484747886657715, "clueweb22-en0036-07-07881:7": 9.454145431518555, "clueweb22-en0016-43-11572:3": 9.292561531066895, "clueweb22-en0034-55-00935:16": 9.272061347961426}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "ref