{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_false-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You are right to consider the effects of cold water on recovery, as there are several approaches with varying outcomes. Cold water immersion is widely used by athletes for post-workout recovery because it can help reduce inflammation and aid performance, as seen with strongman athletes. Cold baths (as opposed to ice baths) may also provide a cooling sensation without numbing, which some find beneficial for recovery.\n\nHowever, using ice or very cold water specifically for soreness does not appear to reduce muscle soreness. In fact, icing mainly numbs pain by reducing blood flow to the area, but its effectiveness in speeding up recovery is uncertain. For general well-being, cold showers can boost alertness and circulation, and may help with muscle recovery, but the evidence for their impact on reducing soreness is mixed.\n\nGiven your preference for warm weather, you might find contrast baths (alternating between warm and cold water) helpful, as they can enhance circulation and reduce soreness, while providing a more comfortable experience. Warm-up intensity can also be adjusted to how strong you feel on the day. If you prefer to avoid cold, focusing on gentle movement, swimming in warmer water, or yoga may suit you best during your recovery.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0008-86-05477:8": 22.738676071166992, "clueweb22-en0004-22-04890:17": 22.731443405151367, "clueweb22-en0040-69-08760:2": 22.519973754882812, "clueweb22-en0022-17-13987:14": 22.28620147705078, "clueweb22-en0001-63-00419:1": 22.227100372314453, "clueweb22-en0031-91-17571:3": 22.178905487060547, "clueweb22-en0001-77-03399:4": 22.113496780395508, "clueweb22-en0010-90-05991:13": 22.088472366333008, "clueweb22-en0008-92-17525:26": 22.08481788635254, "clueweb22-en0008-43-12717:3": 21.80179786682129}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I prefer warm weather for all my outdo