{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "agg_false-qrec-mse", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You are unlikely to \"run out\" of milk for your baby simply from occasional moderate alcohol consumption. Research shows that drinking alcohol does not increase milk production; in fact, it can temporarily reduce the amount of milk released by inhibiting oxytocin, which is essential for milk let-down. If alcohol is consumed in large amounts or very frequently, it may lead to a reduction in milk supply and could impact your baby's feeding and growth.\n\nHowever, occasional moderate drinking is considered safe and does not require you to stop breastfeeding. Babies usually compensate for any temporary reduction by feeding more at later times, and the overall nutritional quality of your milk remains unchanged. The most effective way to maintain or increase your milk supply is to breastfeed frequently, as this stimulates production.\n\nIn summary, moderate, occasional alcohol intake is unlikely to significantly affect your milk supply. Regular or heavy drinking, however, can be problematic for both milk production and your baby's development.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-53-11196:9": 19.90401840209961, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 19.811418533325195, "clueweb22-en0004-28-10513:8": 19.42010498046875, "clueweb22-en0014-46-02684:1": 19.322969436645508, "clueweb22-en0004-28-10513:4": 19.28243064880371, "clueweb22-en0004-28-10513:14": 19.20603370666504, "clueweb22-en0014-46-02684:11": 19.201417922973633, "clueweb22-en0014-46-02684:7": 19.18374252319336, "clueweb22-en0036-86-10058:3": 19.107454299926758, "clueweb22-en0038-30-16467:1": 19.0270938873291}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I prefer foods with more nutrients and protein.", "I am interested in low-carb food."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0004-53-11196:9": 19.90401840209961, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 19.811418533325195, "clueweb22-en0004-28-10513:8": 19.4201049804